{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqgs5b1nrec","workspace":"D:\\zonoid","title":"Orchestrator graph WRITE ops silently accept bare native task indices and create phantom nodes — always pass the full session-uuid/N key","summary":"FOOTGUN hit 2026-06-16 building the note-decay feature. configure_task / block_task / add_dependency / record_decision wires_to accept a BARE native task index ('1'..'12') and return ok, but the write lands on a PHANTOM node literally keyed '1', NOT the real session-scoped graph node (e.g. 'd42ebe37-ee9f-46b6-ae21-f6f82464d482/1'). The READ ops (get_task_detail / suggest_links) correctly reject the bare index with 'unknown task', so the mismatch stays invisible until you read back: despite a dozen 'ok' block/wire/config calls, the real tasks showed status:ready, deps:[], blocked:null, repo:null — i.e. the whole feature DAG was UNHELD under an active dispatch loop, risking premature spawn. FIX: resolve the canonical key FIRST (next_action returns wire[].key as the full id; get_task_detail confirms) and pass the full '<session-uuid>/N' to every orchestrator-graph WRITE. DEEPER BUG: the silent-accept is a daemon defect — write ops should reject an unknown key the same way the read ops do, instead of fabricating a phantom node. Side effect: phantom nodes '1'..'12' now exist as junk edge-endpoints and should be cleaned up.","created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-16T15:11:26.507Z","vec":[-0.08108533173799515,0.04050029069185257,0.03419483080506325,0.05856501683592796,-0.014097006060183048,-0.11066005378961563,-0.0002572126977611333,-0.002977905096486211,-0.01039220578968525,0.07689619064331055,-0.031117679551243782,-0.006372307892888784,0.003701352048665285,-0.059919774532318115,-0.019109558314085007,0.008600684814155102,0.0944044440984726,-0.0659179836511612,0.008966554887592793,0.007859636098146439,0.020618503913283348,0.033437203615903854,-0.001817972050048411,-0.02161121740937233,0.015815369784832,-0.026035459712147713,-0.11462236195802689,